# TallyFlow production environment.
#
#   cp prod.env.example prod.env      # then fill in every value marked REQUIRED
#   docker compose --env-file prod.env up -d --build
#
# prod.env holds real credentials. It is gitignored; keep it that way. One of
# the values below -- TALLYFLOW_SECRET_KEYS -- must also exist somewhere that
# is not this server and not the same cloud account. It is marked.

# --- Public hostnames -----------------------------------------------------
# Three names, one backend. Each needs an A record pointing at this host, with
# ports 80 and 443 open, or Caddy cannot complete the ACME challenge and will
# never obtain a certificate for it. Caddy serves the ones that do resolve and
# keeps retrying the rest, so a missing record is a partial outage rather than
# a stack that refuses to start.
#
# See ./Caddyfile for why all three proxy /v1 to the same API.

# The marketing site and /downloads. The public face.
TALLYFLOW_SITE_HOST=tallyflow.theshubhanshu.dev

# The API and the connector WebSocket.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE THAT CANNOT CHANGE CHEAPLY. It is compiled into the connector
# installer (tallyflow-connector.iss, tally_connector/config.py) and the mobile
# app. Changing it later means re-issuing both to everyone who already
# installed them, so settle it before the first customer.
TALLYFLOW_API_HOST=api-tallyflow.theshubhanshu.dev

# The management portal. Staff and channel partners only.
TALLYFLOW_PORTAL_HOST=pd-tallyflow.theshubhanshu.dev

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by the postgres container. Generate the password, do not invent one:
#   python -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))"
POSTGRES_USER=tallyflow
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=                      # <-- REQUIRED
POSTGRES_DB=tallyflow

# TALLYFLOW_DATABASE_URL is NOT set here on purpose. docker-compose.yml builds
# it from the three values above, so the password exists in exactly one place.
#
# It used to be repeated here, and the two copies diverge in a way that is
# genuinely hard to diagnose: POSTGRES_PASSWORD applies only at initdb, so once
# the volume exists it cannot be changed by editing this file, while the URL can
# -- and then the migrator fails with InvalidPasswordError against a database
# that reports Healthy.
#
# Generate the password rather than inventing one. A password containing @ / : #
# or % has to be percent-encoded inside a URL, and token_urlsafe never emits
# characters that need it.

# Sized for a small box, not for the library default. Postgres is running in a
# 640 MB container alongside the API on the same host; every idle pooled
# connection is memory that the page cache would otherwise use for
# voucher_records. 10 + 5 is ample for one uvicorn worker whose fast path is a
# snapshot read.
#
# Raise these when moving to a managed database with real memory, not before.
TALLYFLOW_DB_POOL_SIZE=10
TALLYFLOW_DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=5

# --- Environment ----------------------------------------------------------
# `prod` also disables /docs and /openapi.json and the debug error bodies. If
# you can still load /docs, this variable did not take effect.
TALLYFLOW_ENVIRONMENT=prod
TALLYFLOW_DEBUG=false

# --- Secrets --------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate both, once:
#   python -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#
# They fail differently and both failures are quiet:
#
#   JWT_SECRET unset -> the backend invents one per process. The symptom is not
#   an error; it is users being signed out on every restart.
TALLYFLOW_JWT_SECRET=                   # <-- REQUIRED, 32+ chars

#   SECRET_KEYS unset -> connector secrets fall back to being encrypted with the
#   JWT secret, so rotating that silently orphans the entire connector fleet.
#
#   *** KEEP A COPY OFF THIS SERVER AND OUT OF THIS CLOUD ACCOUNT. ***
#   It encrypts pairing secrets at rest. Losing it means every customer
#   re-pairs their Tally PC, and no backup in the world helps -- the database
#   dump contains the ciphertext, this value is the key. That separation is the
#   entire point of it being a separate variable.
#
#   Comma-separated, newest first; older keys are retained for decryption
#   during a rotation.
TALLYFLOW_SECRET_KEYS=                  # <-- REQUIRED

# --- Backups --------------------------------------------------------------
# There are none. Postgres runs on this host's disk and nothing copies it
# anywhere.
#
# What that costs if the volume dies: every connector pairing, every approved
# subscription, every user account. TallyPrime can rebuild the voucher data and
# nothing else -- those tables exist here and nowhere on earth. Restoring means
# every customer re-pairing their Tally PC and somebody re-deciding every
# subscription from memory.
#
# Acceptable while this is a pilot you can afford to rebuild by hand. Not
# acceptable once you are charging for it.

# --- Management portal ----------------------------------------------------
# Signups do not provision themselves. A new customer installs everything and
# signs in to an account entitled to nothing -- zero users, zero companies --
# until somebody at https://pd-tallyflow.theshubhanshu.dev approves it and says
# what it covers.
#
# Portal accounts are never self-service, so the first one has to come from
# here. It is created once, on the next start, and only when no portal account
# exists at all; a restart never resets the password of a live account back to
# whatever this file still says. The seeded owner is forced to change it at
# first sign-in for exactly that reason: this value is readable by anyone who
# can read this file.
#
# Blank both once the first owner exists and has signed in.
#   python -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(18))"
TALLYFLOW_PORTAL_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=
TALLYFLOW_PORTAL_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=

# What the approval form pre-fills. Suggestions only -- the real ceilings are
# agreed per customer and stored on their account.
TALLYFLOW_DEFAULT_MAX_COMPANIES=3
TALLYFLOW_DEFAULT_MAX_USERS=5

# --- Diagnostics ----------------------------------------------------------
# The portal's support view. Every captured line goes to an in-memory ring for
# the live tail; only WARNING and above is written down and survives a redeploy.
TALLYFLOW_LOG_CAPTURE_LEVEL=INFO
TALLYFLOW_LOG_PERSIST_LEVEL=WARNING
TALLYFLOW_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Logs customers' connectors push over the socket they already hold open. Off
# makes the backend ignore those frames; connectors keep sending them and
# nothing breaks, which is what makes it safe to flip during an incident.
TALLYFLOW_CONNECTOR_LOGS_ENABLED=true
TALLYFLOW_CONNECTOR_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=14

# --- Scale ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Blank is correct here: one API container, so there is nothing to route
# between. Needed only when a second replica exists -- and a second replica is
# the point at which this single-box deployment stops being the right shape.
TALLYFLOW_REDIS_URL=

# --- Connector fleet ------------------------------------------------------
TALLYFLOW_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
TALLYFLOW_MAX_JOBS_PER_CONNECTOR=2
TALLYFLOW_DEFAULT_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
TALLYFLOW_HEAVY_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180

# --- Freshness ------------------------------------------------------------
TALLYFLOW_SNAPSHOT_STALE_AFTER_SECONDS=900
TALLYFLOW_MIN_REFRESH_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
TALLYFLOW_REFRESH_WORKER_ENABLED=true
TALLYFLOW_REFRESH_WORKER_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
TALLYFLOW_REFRESH_BATCH_SIZE=25

# --- History sync ---------------------------------------------------------
# The safety knob. Asking a desktop TallyPrime for years of vouchers in one
# export is what wedges it, and that PC is usually the shop's till.
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_CHUNK_MONTHS=3
# 1, not 4: only the currently-open financial year is reliably reachable (see
# the known limitation in UAT.md), so asking for more buys nothing and spends
# the customer's Tally on windows that return nothing. Raise it when that
# limitation is lifted -- and note that it is the main driver of database size.
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_MAX_HISTORY_YEARS=1
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_CHUNK_PAUSE_SECONDS=3
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_CHUNK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_INVENTORY_DAYS=400
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_DELTA_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_RECONCILE_DAYS=90
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
TALLYFLOW_SYNC_AUTO_START=true

# --- Public statistics -----------------------------------------------------
# The marketing site's hero prints four aggregate counts, fetched from
# GET /v1/public/stats. It is the only route in the product that reads customer
# data without a token; nothing per-customer is exposed. Off makes the endpoint
# 404 and the site stops drawing the row, with no rebuild needed.
TALLYFLOW_PUBLIC_STATS_ENABLED=true
TALLYFLOW_PUBLIC_STATS_TTL_SECONDS=60
TALLYFLOW_PUBLIC_STATS_ONLINE_WINDOW_SECONDS=300

# --- HTTP -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated, not JSON. Only needed if a browser calls the API from a
# DIFFERENT origin. Native apps do not send Origin, and all three hostnames
# proxy /v1 to the API themselves -- so the site and the portal are each
# same-origin with the API they call and nothing here is required.
#
# Leave it blank. Needing an entry here means something started calling the
# API cross-origin, which for the portal would turn its token into a
# cross-origin credential -- see the note at the top of ./Caddyfile.
TALLYFLOW_CORS_ORIGINS=
TALLYFLOW_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=120
TALLYFLOW_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=10

# --- Releases and updates -------------------------------------------------
# Blank finds /srv/downloads/manifest.json, which docker-compose already mounts,
# so the normal deployment needs nothing here. Blank and not found means version
# checks are simply off -- a missing manifest must never be able to lock the
# fleet out of their own books.
TALLYFLOW_RELEASE_MANIFEST_PATH=

# The two kill switches, both default true.
#
# ENFORCE_MIN_APP_VERSION=false is what to reach for if a floor published by
# mistake has locked users out. It restores service immediately without waiting
# for a corrected manifest to reach every phone.
#
# PUSH_CONNECTOR_UPDATES=false stops the backend telling connectors to install a
# new build over the socket. They fall back to their own six-hourly manifest
# poll, so updates still happen, just slowly.
TALLYFLOW_ENFORCE_MIN_APP_VERSION=true
TALLYFLOW_PUSH_CONNECTOR_UPDATES=true
